artifact.self_contained_problem:
Algorithmic (Kolmogorov) complexity assigns to a finite binary string x the length K(x) of a shortest program (in a fixed universal Turing machine) that outputs x; it measures the descriptive/information content of x independently of the resources used to produce it. Computational complexity, by contrast, classifies problems (or strings, viewed as inputs to decision/search problems) according to the time, space, or other resource bounds required by algorithms solving them, giving rise to classes such as P, NP, PSPACE, BPP, etc., and to resource-bounded notions of complexity for individual strings (e.g., time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity K^t(x)).

Develop a single unified mathematical theory that simultaneously encompasses both algorithmic (descriptional) complexity and computational (resource-bounded) complexity. Specifically: provide a framework in which one can define, for every finite object, a single complexity measure (or family of measures linked by a common formalism) such that:

(a) in the limit of unbounded computational resources, the measure reduces to (prefix or plain) Kolmogorov complexity;
(b) under suitable resource constraints, the measure recovers standard computational complexity classes and resource-bounded Kolmogorov complexity;
(c) the framework yields meaningful theorems (invariance, coding, symmetry of information, hierarchy, completeness) that specialize to the known theorems in each of the two existing theories.

Either construct such a unifying theory and prove that it satisfies (a)–(c), or prove that no theory satisfying these requirements can exist.

